title expect paths carry the same escapes names do

# An expect path is a PATH, so it needs the same decoding a leaf
# does -- otherwise it could never match a decoded name, and the two
# would silently never meet.
#
# Conflict arguments are deliberately NOT decoded. A conflict kind is
# drawn from a closed vocabulary of five words, where an escape
# cannot make anything more expressible and could only admit new
# spellings of a word that has exactly one.
#
# OVER-ESCAPING IS PERMITTED, and the two accepted lines below pin
# it. mtree's encoding is byte-oriented: any byte may be written
# \ooo, so \141 is a legal if pointless spelling of 'a'. \057 falls
# out of the same permission -- it decodes to '/', which in a PATH is
# a legal character, so it is an odd spelling of a legal path rather
# than a special case. A leaf rejects the same escape only because
# '/' is illegal CONTENT there, and that check runs on the decoded
# byte rather than on the spelling. One rule: decode, then validate
# what was decoded.
#
# The space message names the directive because the remedy differs by
# directive -- a space in a path is under-escaped, while a space in a
# conflict kind is simply wrong. It fires on a missing argument too,
# where "takes one path" still reads correctly.

tree base
  1-1: /
    2-100: eq\075uals
    3-100: abc

expect quarantined /eq\075uals
expect quarantined /a\057b
expect quarantined /\141bc
expect quarantined /a\400b
expect quarantined /a\slash
expect quarantined /a b
expect quarantined
